## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/services/contentstack.ts** (`options`): This is where the Contentstack SDK Stack is initialized. The `options` object passed to `contentstack.Stack()` (line 69) lacks the `live_preview` config property that the Contentstack JavaScript SDK supports. Without it, the Stack never registers for live preview, and no query can resolve draft/unpublished content — which is the root cause of the entire feature gap. All 236+ components downstream via useContent → ContentstackContext → pageService → getEntry/getSingleEntry/findSingletonEntry are affected without any component-level change needed.
  - **Minimal fix:** Add `live_preview` config to the `options` object (after line 59, before the conditional fetchOptions block). Add three new env vars to the destructured `process.env` block at lines 17-24: CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_HOST, CONTENTSTACK_MANAGEMENT_TOKEN, CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_ENABLED. Then add to the `options` object: `...(CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_ENABLED === 'true' ? { live_preview: { enable: true, host: CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_HOST, management_token: CONTENTSTACK_MANAGEMENT_TOKEN } } : {})`. The Contentstack SDK's `Stack.livePreviewQuery({ live_preview: <hash>, content_type_uid: <uid> })` method (on the `contentstack.Stack` instance imported at line 3) must be called before content fetches when a live_preview query param is present — wire this in the page-level getStaticProps/getServerSideProps functions (e.g. src/pages/[[...slug]].tsx) by reading `context.query.live_preview` and `context.query.content_type_uid` and calling `Stack.livePreviewQuery(...)` before the existing `getEntry`/`getSingleEntry` calls.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- With a draft (unpublished) edit saved in Contentstack for a page's content, opening that page in the application's preview experience displays the draft text on the rendered page instead of the previously published text.
- After a content author saves a further edit to the same entry in Contentstack, the rendered page reflects the newly saved draft content when the page is refreshed or re-rendered, without redeploying or restarting the application.
- The draft-preview behaviour is observable on the rendered pages of each of the GO, UP, and MX brand sites: a draft edit saved for each brand's content appears on that brand's page in preview.
- Draft content renders in preview across different page types and components (e.g., home page, product pages, content pages) without any per-page or per-component setup by a developer.
- When the site is viewed normally (outside of preview), visitors see the currently published content values on the rendered pages, with no draft or unpublished text visible.
- In the preview experience, the specific draft field values the author edited (e.g., the edited headline and body copy) are visibly present on the rendered page in the locations where that content normally appears.

